1
00:00:00,558 --> 00:00:01,348
Hey everyone!

2
00:00:01,838 --> 00:00:07,460
Today I want to talk about photonic logic gates and focus on the principles behind how they work.

3
00:00:08,014 --> 00:00:14,980
As you may know, photonic computing is currently a pretty hot topic, especially with all the photonic computing hardware coming to the market.

4
00:00:15,342 --> 00:00:22,180
But at the basis of this technology are simple logic operations that can be created using optical principles.

5
00:00:22,542 --> 00:00:28,356
So, what I want to do in this video is explain how these logic operations work on a very fundamental level.

6
00:00:29,358 --> 00:00:38,500
But before we go into that, I first want to mention that you can do every simulation shown in this video yourself using the software that I used to make this video.

7
00:00:38,926 --> 00:00:48,880
The animations were made using a freeware program called Wavedude Next, and this is actually a highly improved version of the program I made myself to create video animations.

8
00:00:49,166 --> 00:00:51,940
Let's call this one Wavedude Classic from now on.

9
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:59,652
I must admit I was pretty smug with the classic version, however, not everyone was equally impressed by the user interface.

10
00:00:59,886 --> 00:01:03,460
For example, my longtime subscriber Alexander Greef.

11
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:11,812
And even though his comment, of course, seriously dented my fragile ego, he meant well because he also offered to improve the interface.

12
00:01:12,494 --> 00:01:16,932
At some point we joined forces and we added a ton of additional functionality.

13
00:01:17,230 --> 00:01:22,960
I say we, but Alex did almost all the hard work and I well contributed a lot of deep thoughts.

14
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:29,348
Anyway, this resulted in Wave Dude Next, and links to the software can be found in the description.

15
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:35,044
It is not only useful to get a better understanding of optics but any kind of wave phenomenon really.

16
00:01:36,654 --> 00:01:40,740
Okay, let's return to the subject of this video: photonic logic gates.

17
00:01:41,134 --> 00:01:48,260
Now, logic is all about relationships between inputs and outputs, you probably know these so-called truth tables from electronics.

18
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:55,844
You have a device with two inputs A and B, and one output Q that contains the logical result of a specific operation.

19
00:01:56,206 --> 00:01:58,500
Zero means false and one means true.

20
00:01:58,766 --> 00:02:04,932
And so for an AND gate you need both A and B to be true for the operation to result in the value true.

21
00:02:05,326 --> 00:02:18,532
For an OR operation, this is the case if either A or B or both are true, and in the example of an exclusive OR gate, the difference between the OR gate is that when both are true, output Q is actually false.

22
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,796
So basically it excludes the AND condition.

23
00:02:23,246 --> 00:02:29,040
As I will show, you can also make these logical elements using light based on its wave properties.

24
00:02:29,454 --> 00:02:32,420
So let's take the exclusive OR gate as an example.

25
00:02:33,006 --> 00:02:37,476
On the left side are the inputs A and B that insert light into two fibers.

26
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:42,820
In the logic unit, the two signals approach each other closely in this region here.

27
00:02:43,342 --> 00:02:47,748
On the right side we see an output, Q that contains the required logic result.

28
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:56,932
There is also this second output where we dump all the light that we don't use for the logic operation, and I decided to name this output R for residual.

29
00:02:57,390 --> 00:03:00,400
This port can in principle also perform useful tasks.

30
00:03:00,654 --> 00:03:07,040
You can, for example, some signals on this port, but in this video I want to focus on output port Q.

31
00:03:08,366 --> 00:03:15,908
Let me quickly demonstrate how an optical exclusive OR works, and I will be showing you the field and the intensity in two separate images.

32
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:22,756
If there is no light going into A and B, there is obviously also no field and no output on Q.

33
00:03:23,342 --> 00:03:33,636
If we switch on either A or B, there is an output on Q, and if you watch carefully, the intensity is equally distributed over Q and the residual in both cases.

34
00:03:34,222 --> 00:03:43,108
However, if you switch A and B on together at the same time, output Q goes dark and all the energy is rerouted into the residual port.

35
00:03:44,174 --> 00:03:45,680
Now that's pretty amazing, right?

36
00:03:45,966 --> 00:03:50,564
You're sending in more light and somehow none of it reaches the exclusive OR output.

37
00:03:51,246 --> 00:03:56,708
And the other weird thing is that the two optical tracks in the device are not directly connected.

38
00:03:57,006 --> 00:03:58,500
So what's going on there?

39
00:03:59,438 --> 00:04:04,484
Well, let's take a closer look at both phenomena in a few simplified configurations.

40
00:04:06,414 --> 00:04:13,424
Let's start with a single light conductor or fiber in a medium with a refractive index of in this case 1.4.

41
00:04:13,966 --> 00:04:21,348
So the fiber is very thin and only supports a single wave mode, meaning that there is no space to facilitate two waves next to each other.

42
00:04:22,254 --> 00:04:25,796
The fiber also has a higher refractive index than the surrounding medium.

43
00:04:25,998 --> 00:04:31,584
In this case, I set it to 1.8, and on the left side I have placed a source in the center of the fiber.

44
00:04:32,064 --> 00:04:35,748
this is actually a coherent source with one specific frequency.

45
00:04:36,558 --> 00:04:43,460
The left end of the fiber is also embedded in a reflective medium to avoid the wave exiting the fiber directly around the source.

46
00:04:44,110 --> 00:04:46,468
So let's continue the simulation.

47
00:04:48,078 --> 00:04:54,980
From both the field and the intensity animations, you can observe that virtually all of the wave energy is guided through the fiber.

48
00:04:55,470 --> 00:05:00,384
Here is the intensity result, and I added an intensity probe inside the fiber.

49
00:05:00,782 --> 00:05:03,236
And the graph shows a virtually constant value.

50
00:05:03,886 --> 00:05:08,516
So an obvious question you might have: why doesn't the wave escape from the fiber?

51
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:12,228
Now the answer is actually kind of surprising.

52
00:05:12,558 --> 00:05:24,932
The field itself is actually not confined to the inside of the fiber, but extends quite a bit beyond the interface, and the part that is outside the fiber is actually the part that helps keeping the energy confined.

53
00:05:25,326 --> 00:05:26,820
So let me try to explain.

54
00:05:28,270 --> 00:05:38,564
Here is the same fiber, but in this case I placed an intensity probe across the fiber, and the dotted lines in the intensity plot indicate approximately where the fiber interfaces are.

55
00:05:39,246 --> 00:05:45,188
And there is a significant tail sticking out of the fiber that fades away quickly when moving further from the interface.

56
00:05:46,064 --> 00:05:54,020
The part of the field outside the fiber interface is called the evanescent field, named after the rock band, which was named after the phenomenon.

57
00:05:54,926 --> 00:05:55,716
Nah, just kidding.

58
00:05:56,398 --> 00:05:59,844
It refers to the part of the field that fades quickly outside the fiber.

59
00:06:01,038 --> 00:06:10,788
It just travels along with the light inside the fiber, but somehow no energy escapes, and so the intensity stays the same inside the fiber as the field propagates.

60
00:06:12,238 --> 00:06:17,316
Now to at least intuitively understand this phenomenon, I want you to keep two things in mind.

61
00:06:17,710 --> 00:06:21,732
One, the field of a wave cannot be discontinuous.

62
00:06:22,158 --> 00:06:31,492
So if there is some kind of boundary, the wave does not end with some kind of discontinuity but adapts to the boundary in a way that reflects the wave physics.

63
00:06:31,726 --> 00:06:37,188
And for example, on a reflective interface, the field intensity will gradually go to zero at the interface.

64
00:06:38,926 --> 00:06:39,780
And 2.

65
00:06:40,238 --> 00:06:48,780
The energy in a wave always propagates perpendicular to what is called the wavefront, which is a set of points in the field that share a common phase.

66
00:06:49,518 --> 00:06:56,708
Now the easiest way to explain what happens in the case of the effinescent field is to consider the shape of the wavefront.

67
00:06:58,574 --> 00:07:09,124
So the surrounding medium has a lower refractive index, and this causes waves to travel faster, meaning that the part of the wavefront that surrounds the fiber wants to sort of run ahead.

68
00:07:09,422 --> 00:07:14,020
But since the field cannot be discontinuous, the actual wavefront will look something like this.

69
00:07:14,798 --> 00:07:18,180
For illustration, the curvature is a bit exaggerated here.

70
00:07:18,766 --> 00:07:26,948
Now considering that wave energy is always transported perpendicular to the wavefront, it will be redirected towards the fiber again.

71
00:07:27,502 --> 00:07:31,108
And so that is total internal reflection in terms of wave behavior.

72
00:07:31,566 --> 00:07:39,172
The wave energy crossing the boundary is continuously steered to the side where the refractive index is higher due to the shape of the wavefront.

73
00:07:39,630 --> 00:07:44,516
And this phenomenon creates the evanescent field and keeps the energy from escaping the fiber.

74
00:07:46,478 --> 00:07:54,052
Earlier I showed that in an exclusive OR gate light would cross over between two fibers that are not overlapping or in direct contact.

75
00:07:54,542 --> 00:07:56,004
So how is that possible?

76
00:07:56,462 --> 00:08:01,156
Well it turns out that the evanescent field also plays a large role in this process.

77
00:08:01,678 --> 00:08:08,708
The part of the field outside a fiber can reach and penetrate a neighboring fiber, and this can lead to energy transfer.

78
00:08:09,358 --> 00:08:11,652
So let me show you how this works in a bit more detail.

79
00:08:12,494 --> 00:08:19,556
Here I added a second fiber, B, which is pretty close to the first one, A, say within a wavelength of distance or so.

80
00:08:19,886 --> 00:08:23,268
And if we now start the simulation, you see something amazing happening.

81
00:08:23,662 --> 00:08:29,156
The field crosses over to fiber B almost completely and as if there is no boundary.

82
00:08:30,126 --> 00:08:31,044
So what is going on?

83
00:08:31,758 --> 00:08:50,948
Well the evanescent field of fiber A now reaches B and suddenly a part of it finds itself propagating slower again, causing the wavefront to curve, but this time into the fiber B, and this effectively drains the energy from the evanescent field, and with that the energy of the field in fiber A.

84
00:08:52,142 --> 00:09:09,892
Here I have schematically indicated the direction of the global wavefront in the two fibers together, and given that the wave energy travels perpendicular to the wavefront, energy will transfer from A to B, and surprisingly enough, the energy of fiber A empties almost completely into B.

85
00:09:11,374 --> 00:09:13,924
Let's now put the fibers even closer together.

86
00:09:15,342 --> 00:09:20,740
And in this case, the energy transfer between fibers occurs over much shorter distances.

87
00:09:21,422 --> 00:09:31,908
The reason is that the evanescent field fades very fast outside a fiber, and the size of the gap matters enormously for how quickly energy can cross over to the other side.

88
00:09:33,088 --> 00:09:45,808
Strangely enough, as long as the fibers keep running side by side, the light will keep going back and forth between fibers A and B, and the distance required for one full transfer is called the coupling length.

89
00:09:47,054 --> 00:10:07,556
You might expect this process to settle down, eventually, with the energy spreading evenly over both fibers, but in an ideal situation with identical fibers, no losses and truly monochromatic, coherent light, this process will go on forever, and it's this predictability that we can use to make optical logic devices.

90
00:10:08,430 --> 00:10:23,172
Because what we can do now is bend the two paths away from each other at exactly the right point, for example where the maximum amount of field has crossed over, and by doing this we can lock the interference pattern created in the two fibers.

91
00:10:23,630 --> 00:10:30,564
In this case, all the energy in fiber A was transferred before the split, causing output Q to end up empty.

92
00:10:32,654 --> 00:10:41,088
This works the same way in the other direction when only source B is on, and now all of the energy is transferred into fiber A and ends up in Q.

93
00:10:42,190 --> 00:10:46,916
And if we switch on both inputs, the combined field goes to both exits.

94
00:10:47,246 --> 00:10:52,292
I said combined because it is not really clear anymore which light ended up at which end.

95
00:10:53,806 --> 00:10:57,892
Now unfortunately, this configuration does not represent a logic gate yet.

96
00:10:58,446 --> 00:11:12,516
You could get exactly the same behavior by simply using straight light conductors and then switch the definitions of the output ports, and that would actually be way simpler and it does not require any interference to create a crossover.

97
00:11:13,166 --> 00:11:18,308
So let's now turn this configuration into a true logic OR gate in a few steps.

98
00:11:18,894 --> 00:11:25,860
The first thing we do is slightly widen the gap between A and B such that the coupling length becomes twice as long.

99
00:11:26,190 --> 00:11:27,364
And here is what happens now.

100
00:11:28,046 --> 00:11:33,636
If input A is switched on, the energy is now equally divided over the two outputs.

101
00:11:34,158 --> 00:11:37,508
And the same thing happens if you switch on source B.

102
00:11:39,950 --> 00:11:42,660
And here is the situation with both sources on.

103
00:11:43,022 --> 00:11:48,260
We also have signal on exit Q, so in principle we have created an OR gate.

104
00:11:48,814 --> 00:11:53,924
But even in this case, there is no reason to use wave interference to get to this result.

105
00:11:54,670 --> 00:12:03,908
The only thing is that the output signal on Q is now two times as high when both A and B are on, compared to when only A or B is on.

106
00:12:04,622 --> 00:12:08,080
And the difference I mentioned is actually in the scaling on the y-axis.

107
00:12:08,270 --> 00:12:12,996
I wasn't sure the scale would be visible on your device, so I added additional indicators for the scale.

108
00:12:13,326 --> 00:12:18,372
But you can see that the levels with A and B on are indeed now twice as high.

109
00:12:18,926 --> 00:12:24,004
So from a digital signal perspective, this is not really a very neat OR gate.

110
00:12:24,718 --> 00:12:27,140
But we can make it more neat using a little trick.

111
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:37,252
We can redirect some of that excess intensity to the residual output, and we can do that without changing anything in the configuration of the junction as it is shown here.

112
00:12:37,710 --> 00:12:43,396
So let me first show you what the energy transfer looks like and then explain how this works.

113
00:12:48,238 --> 00:12:55,876
So if you now look at the outputs, approximately a quarter is directed to the logic output and three quarters to the residual.

114
00:12:56,206 --> 00:13:06,212
And again, the scaling in these graphs isn't really optimal, but the output level at Q is now approximately at the same level as when only one of the inputs is on.

115
00:13:06,830 --> 00:13:14,436
So now we got a nicely behaving OR logic output at Q with constant levels for any of the true conditions.

116
00:13:16,078 --> 00:13:18,820
The main question is of course, what did I change?

117
00:13:19,342 --> 00:13:23,524
Well, I delayed the phase of input B with respect to input A.

118
00:13:23,918 --> 00:13:29,572
And in the simulation I changed the phase in the source settings because it is a property of the source.

119
00:13:30,030 --> 00:13:33,732
But you could also achieve a phase delay in the device configuration itself.

120
00:13:34,254 --> 00:13:39,108
For example, by delaying the second input using a patch with a high refractive index.

121
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:44,900
Or create a slightly longer path for signal B before it arrives at the junction.

122
00:13:45,326 --> 00:13:56,800
Anyway, what changing the phase allows you to do is to manipulate the common wavefront, and by creating a slight tilt in the wavefront, you can distribute the wave energy over the two ports as needed.

123
00:13:57,134 --> 00:14:11,364
So in this case I delayed the phase of the input B by around 30 degrees, and the result is that only a quarter of the total intensity goes into Q, which brings its output level to the same level of when only a single input is on.

124
00:14:13,262 --> 00:14:14,820
So that is an OR gate.

125
00:14:15,086 --> 00:14:19,236
Now let's take this only a small step further and create an exclusive OR gate.

126
00:14:19,790 --> 00:14:25,700
The main difference being that when both inputs A and B are on, Q needs to be completely off.

127
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:28,068
So here you see how that works.

128
00:14:32,782 --> 00:14:37,508
Now given what I have just told you, it shouldn't be hard to guess what was changed.

129
00:14:38,350 --> 00:14:44,452
Well the only change I made with respect to the OR gate is delay the phase of the input B a bit further.

130
00:14:44,878 --> 00:14:50,276
And I increased the value from around minus 30 degrees to minus 90 degrees.

131
00:14:50,990 --> 00:14:57,956
So what happens now is that the field in B, because it lags behind way more, drains intensity from A much faster.

132
00:14:58,222 --> 00:15:05,092
And now effectively all the intensity ends up in the residual gate, which leaves nothing for the exclusive OR gate.

133
00:15:05,902 --> 00:15:11,108
And so here is our truth table with a summary of all the respective wave configurations.

134
00:15:12,782 --> 00:15:23,716
Now, in my personal opinion, this is one of the coolest things of photonic logic that you can change which exit light takes in these junctions by tuning the phase differences between inputs.

135
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:29,700
And I hope I was able to explain how wave interference lies at the heart of photonic logic.

136
00:15:30,734 --> 00:15:36,836
By using coherent input signals with specific phase differences, you can create all kinds of logic units.

137
00:15:37,326 --> 00:15:44,720
However, it is probably good to emphasize that with these simple Mach-Sender configurations, you cannot make every type of logical element.

138
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:53,028
For example, an AND, a NOR or an exclusive NOR are not possible because they require output when there is no input.

139
00:15:53,326 --> 00:16:03,012
So for these types of logic elements, you need an additional coherent input that is constantly on in order to create an output signal when both A and B are off.

140
00:16:04,846 --> 00:16:08,676
I want to end with a few remarks on the simulation software I use for this video.

141
00:16:09,134 --> 00:16:17,764
So both Alex and I agree that Wavedude could be a powerful tool for education, in particular in optics or in RF technology.

142
00:16:18,382 --> 00:16:22,116
So we decided that this tool should be free for everyone and open source.

143
00:16:22,414 --> 00:16:33,636
But of course, it is not complete, and many more optics-inspired features could be added, such as different types of gratings, Fresnel lenses, meta surfaces, and various integrated photonic modules.

144
00:16:34,158 --> 00:16:38,852
So let us know in the comments what you would like to have added to the current set of tools.

145
00:16:39,278 --> 00:16:46,692
And when you are into programming, it is also possible to help us implement specific functionality in the code because it is available on GitHub.

146
00:16:47,248 --> 00:16:52,900
Or maybe help port the application to Linux or Mac OS and make it available to more people.

147
00:16:53,530 --> 00:16:54,390
The sky is the limit.

148
00:16:55,086 --> 00:16:57,888
So let's see what direction this project will be going.

